<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <!--
      A WALL-CLOCK CAP ON THE WHOLE SESSION - not a connection timeout.

      The comment that stood here said this "prevents vstest from timing out on
      first connection" and pointed at VSTEST_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT. That is a
      different setting, and believing this one was about connecting is why
      nobody looked at it while it was cutting real runs short.
      TestSessionTimeout kills the run when the clock runs out, and fifteen
      minutes could not finish EITHER leg of this project:

          net9.0,  quiet box         14 m 46 s
          net10.0, quiet box         20 m 11 s
          net9.0,  box under load    28 m 05 s

      So the command CLAUDE.md documents aborted at minute fifteen and printed
      "Failed!" with about 54 tests never run - which reads exactly like a real
      break, and cost most of 2026-10-24 before the cap was found rather than
      the suite blamed.

      Forty-five minutes was 1.6x the worst run above - but the suite kept
      growing, and by 2026-11-16 it had outgrown that cap too. At 6,515 tests a
      leg it ran, to completion under its own parallel load:

          net9.0,  full suite        47 m 14 s
          net10.0, full suite        48 m 19 s

      measured 2026-11-16 (6,515 of 6,515 passed each leg, the cap lifted on the
      CLI so they could finish). So 45 minutes had begun aborting the documented
      command at the clock - the same "Failed!" trap as the fifteen-minute one,
      about a leg's worth of tests never run. Seventy-five minutes is about 1.5x
      the worst above.

      The cap is there to stop a HUNG run holding the box overnight; it is not a
      judgement on how fast this suite ought to be. Raise it again rather than
      trimming tests to fit it - and if it ever trips, read it as "something is
      stuck", because nothing healthy here takes that long.
    -->
    <TestSessionTimeout>4500000</TestSessionTimeout>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
